# pip-audit ignore list for svc-driftless
#
# Format: one vulnerability ID per line. Each entry MUST have a "# reason:"
# comment immediately above explaining why we accept the risk.
#
# Adding to this file is the LAST resort. First try:
#   1. Upgrade the affected package
#   2. Switch to a different package
#   3. Pin a floor version that contains the fix
# Only use --ignore-vuln when the upstream has no fix available
# and the vulnerability is not exploitable in our usage pattern.
#
# EMPTY BY VERIFICATION, not by neglect. CI audits a clean project venv (#1375),
# and this project reports zero vulnerabilities in one. Every entry this file
# used to carry — msgpack, torch — described packages driftless neither declares
# nor imports, reported only because the audit used to scan the self-hosted
# runner's shared environment. Those entries said to drop them once that stopped
# being true. It has.
#
# This list must stay empty as long as it can. pyproject.toml declares real
# runtime dependencies (alembic, fastapi, jinja2, pydantic, python-multipart,
# sqlalchemy), so a CVE in one of THOSE is a genuine finding for this project and
# must be fixed by upgrading or raising the floor — never by adding a line here.
# python-multipart is the worked example: its ReDoS advisories were answered with
# a `>=0.0.18` floor in pyproject, not with an entry here. Anything added must
# fire in a clean venv, and an entry that stops firing is dead and should be
# deleted.
